# The listdetail scene: adaptive list-detail's INVARIANT, asserted at
# whatever width the host hands it (DESIGN.md, Adaptive list-detail).
#
# The sibling of `split`, and its opposite in exactly one respect.
# `split` drives the size class with real resizes and names the
# presentation on each side; a phone or tablet cannot do that at all,
# because the system owns those surfaces (DESIGN.md, Windows). So the
# one scene that gates list-detail was desktop-only, and the two phone
# backends' list-detail arms ran in no lane at all — a wrong Compose or
# SwiftUI arrangement compiled and passed everything.
#
# This file therefore never resizes and never names a presentation. It
# carries the BARE `expect_split`, which asserts the asymmetric
# invariant — a regular window must not show ONE pane while its stack
# holds two — rather than comparing a literal. That claim is true at
# every width a lane can hand it, so the same bytes run on a 320dp
# phone, a 1280dp tablet, and a desktop window nobody sized. It is also
# the reason there is no `back` here: whether back pops is the arm's
# answer, so it differs by width, and this file may only say things
# that are true at all of them.
#
# WHAT EACH LANE GETS. On a compact host the invariant is vacuous and
# the leg rests on the model reads and the real-tree read at the end.
# On a regular host — the iPad, the tablet emulator, a desktop window —
# it bites: the split arm has to have run AND to have said so through
# the platform's own component. Picking a device outside the 400..840
# band is the LANE's job, the same rule check-steps enforces on a
# resize (a portrait medium_tablet is 800dp, squarely inside it).
#
# NO expect_title HERE, deliberately. This scene and `split` are the
# SAME app — one window, one button, one push — and differ only in what
# they may assert. A title assertion is the one claim that cannot be
# shared, because one app has one title, and asserting a different one
# would force a second binary per language for no coverage: `split`
# already checks the title lowering against this very app. Keeping this
# file title-free is what lets every language serve both scenes with
# ONE guest.
expect_entries 0
expect_split
# Push the detail. WHERE it lands is the platform's answer — beside the
# list on a regular window, covering it on a compact one — and neither
# is asserted. Both must satisfy the invariant.
click button#0
expect_entries 1
expect label#0 "list pane"
expect label#last "detail pane"
# THE CLAIM THAT MATTERS, and the one the line above cannot make: with
# an entry on the stack, a regular window owes two panes.
expect_split
# The detail pane is on screen in BOTH arms — the trailing pane on a
# regular window, the covering one on a compact window — so this is the
# one assertion here that reads a REAL tree on every lane. `expect
# label#last` above reads kaya's own model and passes for an arm that
# ran and drew nothing, which is the exact gap that let a
# non-rendering split arm look green.
expect_ax label#last "label/detail pane"
